  "id": "Compound::DB00573 - Gene::5743",
  "verbalization": "Fenoprofen, a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug, is implicated in the interaction with Ptgs2 through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Fenoprofen binds to Ptgs1, which is involved in the prostaglandin-endoperoxide synthase activity, and this activity is linked to Ptgs2. Additionally, Fenoprofen is included in the class of Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Compounds, which includes drugs like Ketorolac and Nabumetone, all of which bind to Ptgs2. This indicates a broader pharmacological relationship where Fenoprofen, through its inclusion in a class of drugs that target cyclooxygenase enzymes, exerts its effects on Ptgs2. The lowest common ancestors of Fenoprofen, Ketorolac, and Nabumetone are shared in the category of Nonsteroidal Antiinflammatory Drugs and Carbonyl Compounds, reinforcing the common mechanism of action. The integration of these pathways highlights a mechanism where Fenoprofen's interaction with Ptgs2 is mediated through its class of drugs, which share a common target and mechanism of action.",
